  "title": "NGC 7129: Star Formation with Jets from Webb",
  "date": "2026-11-16",
  "year": "2026",
  "explanation": "<strong>Explanation:</strong> How do stars form? To help answer this question, <a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/\">NASA</a> used the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/webb/\">James Webb Space Telescope</a> to take the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/asset/webb/ngc-7129-nircam-image/\">featured detailed image</a> of the star-forming region <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NGC_7129\">NGC 7129</a> in <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/ems/07_infraredwaves/\">infrared light</a>. Star formation progresses when <a href=\"https://www.labxchange.org/library/items/lb:LabXchange:bc30bab5:lx_simulation:1\">gravity</a> pulls together massive amounts of gas, but soon things can get complicated when previously formed stars emit powerful <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2023-july-28-young-stars-stellar-jets/\">jets</a> and <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2025-february-3-wolf-rayet-star-124-stellar-wind-machine/\">winds</a> -- and some then <a href=\"https://youtu.be/-yEBVm5o97E\">explode</a>. Opaque <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/universe/dust-in-the-stellar-wind-a-cosmological-primer/\">dust</a> can form in the cool <a href=\"https://www.eso.org/sci/meetings/2024/Bthelines/Ludwig.pdf\">atmospheres of stars</a> and, when dispersed, can obstruct <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/ems/09_visiblelight/\">visible-light</a> images. Many stars can be seen to be forming in the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/asset/webb/ngc-7129-nircam-image/#:~:text=Interactive:\">featured image</a>, with the young stars on the left, in the gold-colored gas, typically being more mature than the young stars on the right -- in the red region. <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2025-may-28-herbig-haro-24/\">Jets</a> and <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2000-march-18-a-wind-from-the-sun/\">wind</a>s likely cause the filamentary nature of the red-colored gas. Studying stars at different stages of development <a href=\"https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1734167121479-6147220c23f0?q=80&amp;w=687&amp;auto=format&amp;fit=crop&amp;ixlib=rb-4.1.0&amp;ixid=NHwxMjA3fDA%3D\">bolsters understanding</a> about what <a href=\"https://scixplorer.org/abs/2020SSRv..216...68G/abstract\">physical processes</a> are most important and when.<br><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture:</strong> sky duel",
